<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Uzbekistan</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Tashkent</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The most populous Central Asian state, heir to Samarkand and Bukhara, which opened up after decades of Islam Karimov's authoritarian rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uzbek-ssr">Uzbek SSR</a> declared independence on 31 August 1991 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-karimov">Islam Karimov</a>, who had led the republic since 1989 and was elected president in December 1991. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> was promoted as a national hero in place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Lenin</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-karimov">Karimov</a> ruled harshly for 25 years, suppressing opposition and Islamist movements; in 2005 security forces killed hundreds of protesters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andijan">Andijan</a>. The state kept tight control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> production, which relied on forced labour, and over currency exchange. Uzbekistan borders all other Central Asian states and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-emirate-of-afghanistan-2021-present">Afghanistan</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-movement-of-uzbekistan">Islamic Movement of Uzbekistan</a> fought the government from bases abroad.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-karimov">Karimov</a>'s death in 2016, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shavkat-mirziyoyev">Shavkat Mirziyoyev</a> became president, freeing some political prisoners, ending forced labour in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> harvest, liberalising the currency and improving relations with neighbours. A 2023 constitution extended presidential terms.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/uzbekistan.jpg" alt="Uzbekistan" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Farhod Yoʻldoshev · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Uzbekistan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tashkent</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Islam Karimov, Shavkat Mirziyoyev</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Uzbek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Uzbekistan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=uzbekistan" aria-label="Uzbekistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6371 704 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Uzbekistan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6691 960L6691 960M6691 960L6444 924"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6691 960h0M6444 924h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6691 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6691 960h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tashkent. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=uzbekistan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
